     1	# Curator Hardened Execution Profile 1.0
     2	
     3	Candidate `hardened-1.0.0-rc.1`. This document is normative. The key words
     4	MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY are used as in
     5	[`protocol/core.md`](core.md) section 1.
     6	
     7	## 0. Status, scope, and relationship to protocol 1.0
     8	
     9	[`protocol/core.md`](core.md) section 4.2.1 defines exactly one execution policy
    10	for the compiled-build drivers, the portable `manager-worker-v1` policy, and
    11	names six guarantees that the portable policy explicitly does not provide. It
    12	reserves the identity `hardened-worker-v1` for a separate profile and defers
    13	those guarantees to that profile.
    14	
    15	This document is that profile. It is **additive and separately versioned**:
    16	
    17	- It does not modify, widen, or reinterpret any rule of protocol `1.0.0-rc.5`.
    18	- It does not change any byte of `conformance/v1`, `schemas/v1`, or
    19	  [`release/1.0.0-rc.5.json`](../release/1.0.0-rc.5.json). The portable suite
    20	  keeps its own pin.
    21	- Its conformance suite root is `conformance/hardened/v1` and its schemas are
    22	  under `schemas/hardened/v1`. Its candidate metadata is
    23	  [`release/hardened-1.0.0-rc.1.json`](../release/hardened-1.0.0-rc.1.json).
    24	- The portable profile remains a complete, self-contained, weaker contract. A
    25	  manager MAY implement the portable profile and never implement this one.
    26	
    27	**No platform is qualified for the hardened profile in this revision.** Section
    28	6 declares, per platform, the capability classes a host MUST provide and the
    29	public primitives a candidate binding would use. Every declaration in this
    30	revision carries `qualification_status: "unqualified"`, because no native
    31	adversarial evidence exists yet. Section 7 therefore has one observable
    32	consequence today: **every host rejects the hardened profile before any package
    33	byte reaches any Go process.** That is the intended fail-closed behavior, not a
    34	gap. Qualification is added by the implementation and verification tasks named
    35	in section 11, each of which MUST supply native adversarial evidence for the
    36	platform it qualifies.
    37	
    38	## 1. Terms
    39	
    40	- **build domain** — the kernel- or hypervisor-enforced containment object that
    41	  holds every process which may observe a package byte. Membership is a
    42	  property the contained processes cannot renounce.
    43	- **hardened supervisor** — the trusted, uncontained, manager-owned process that
    44	  probes capabilities, creates the build domain, and applies every control. It
    45	  is never a package-selected program.
    46	- **domain-root worker** — the first process inside the build domain, and the
    47	  ancestor of every other process in it.
    48	- **guarantee** — one of the six named properties of section 5. A guarantee is
    49	  a statement about what the kernel or hypervisor refuses to do, never about
    50	  what the manager chooses not to do.
    51	- **capability class** — a platform-neutral primitive requirement, defined in
    52	  section 6.1, whose presence is necessary to establish one or more guarantees.
    53	- **enforcement backend** — the concrete, named, per-platform mechanism that
    54	  supplies the capability classes on one host.
    55	- **qualified platform** — a `(platform, minimum version, required
    56	  configuration, enforcement backend)` tuple for which native adversarial
    57	  evidence proves every guarantee. Qualification is a property of this
    58	  specification revision, not of a running host.
    59	- **hardened operation** — an install, update, repair, dry-run, status, or
    60	  garbage-collection operation that has selected the hardened profile.
    61	
    62	## 2. Identities
    63	
    64	### 2.1 Execution-policy identity
    65	
    66	```json
    67	{"execution_policy": "hardened-worker-v1"}
    68	```
    69	
    70	`hardened-worker-v1` is the exact identity reserved by `protocol/core.md`
    71	section 4.2.1 and by the `reserved_hardened_execution_policy` field of
    72	[`conformance/v1/vectors/go-host-execution-policy.json`](../conformance/v1/vectors/go-host-execution-policy.json).
    73	A hardened operation MUST use that identity and MUST NOT introduce another.
    74	
    75	The identity occupies exactly the `execution_policy` slot of the canonical build
    76	policy object defined by `protocol/core.md` sections 9.1 and 9.2. A hardened
    77	build input is byte-for-byte the portable build input for the same source,
    78	command, target, and toolchain with that one value replaced. No field is added
    79	to, removed from, or reordered within the hashed build input.
    80	
    81	That is a deliberate constraint, not an accident of drafting. The rc.5
    82	conformance suite reserved a specific hardened cache key over exactly that
    83	input, and this profile reproduces it rather than inventing a new one:
    84	
    85	```text
    86	cache_key(hardened) = sha256:13736230d33ce59de7f7323dcd4cffd510655ad8dabd5ee9e8b6cb182ec70037
    87	```
    88	
    89	for the reserved example input of
    90	`conformance/v1/vectors/go-host-execution-policy.json#cache_identity`. A
    91	conforming implementation MUST reproduce that key from that input.
    92	
    93	### 2.2 Profile identity
    94	
    95	```json
    96	{"hardened_profile": "hardened-profile-v1"}
    97	```
    98	
    99	The profile identity names this revision's complete contract: the six
   100	guarantees of section 5, the capability classes of section 6, the ordering of
   101	section 7, and the diagnostics of section 10. It is NOT part of the hashed
   102	build input, because it is bound one-to-one to the execution-policy identity:
   103	
   104	- an operation whose execution policy is `hardened-worker-v1` MUST report
   105	  profile identity `hardened-profile-v1`, and
   106	- an operation whose execution policy is `manager-worker-v1` MUST NOT report a
   107	  hardened profile identity at all.
   108	
   109	Violating either direction is `hardened_profile_claim_forbidden`. A future
   110	revision that changes which guarantees hold, which capability classes are
   111	required, or where the failure boundary sits MUST introduce both a new profile
   112	identity and a new execution-policy identity; it MUST NOT reuse
   113	`hardened-worker-v1` with a different guarantee set, and it MUST NOT be enabled
   114	by widening the closed portable `manager-worker-v1` constant.
   115	
   116	### 2.3 Trusted-computing-base identity
   117	
   118	```json
   119	{
   120	  "record_version": "hardened-tcb-v1",
   121	  "hardened_profile": "hardened-profile-v1",
   122	  "execution_policy": "hardened-worker-v1",
   123	  "platform": "linux",
   124	  "enforcement_backend": "linux-namespace-seccomp-v1",
   125	  "supervisor_sha256": "sha256:...",
   126	  "worker_sha256": "sha256:...",
   127	  "toolchain": {"algorithm": "curator-go-toolchain-v1", "content_sha256": "sha256:..."}
   128	}
   129	```
   130	
   131	The TCB record names every mutable component whose compromise defeats the
   132	guarantees: the supervisor bytes, the worker bytes, the enforcement backend, and
   133	the fingerprinted toolchain. It is a closed record; section 8 states exactly
   134	where it may and may not appear.
   135	
   136	An implementation that starts the supervisor or the worker through a mutable
   137	interpreter, an installed package tree, a script, or any other component it does
   138	not fingerprint MUST add those components to this record and MUST say so; it
   139	MUST NOT report a TCB record that is narrower than what it actually trusts.
   140	
   141	### 2.4 Capability-evidence identity
   142	
   143	```json
   144	{"record_version": "hardened-capability-evidence-v1"}
   145	```
   146	
   147	Section 6.4 defines the record. It is a distinct record version from the
   148	portable `capability-evidence-v1` record of `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1;
   149	the two MUST NOT be merged, aliased, or converted into one another.
   150	
   151	### 2.5 Enforcement-backend identity
   152	
   153	An enforcement backend is named by a closed identifier declared in section 6.3.
   154	It is reported in the TCB record and in a hardened conformance claim. It is
   155	never part of a hashed build identity, for the reason given in section 8.3.
   156	
   157	### 2.6 Identity binding summary
   158	
   159	| Identity | Value in this revision | In hashed build input | In receipt bytes | In install marker | In conformance claim | Result-only |
   160	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   161	| execution policy | `hardened-worker-v1` | yes | yes | yes | yes | no |
   162	| profile | `hardened-profile-v1` | no | no | yes | yes | no |
   163	| TCB record | `hardened-tcb-v1` | no | no | yes | yes | no |
   164	| enforcement backend | section 6.3 | no | no | no | yes | no |
   165	| capability evidence | `hardened-capability-evidence-v1` | no | no | no | no | yes |
   166	
   167	## 3. Threat model
   168	
   169	### 3.1 Adversary
   170	
   171	The adversary supplies every package byte: manifest values, build-root contents,
   172	Go source, vendored dependency source, embedded inputs, external repository
   173	contents, and the repository build descriptor. The adversary may also observe
   174	the manager's public behavior and may retry an operation arbitrarily often.
   175	
   176	The adversary is assumed able to:
   177	
   178	- exploit a defect in the Go parser, type checker, compiler, assembler, or
   179	  linker and obtain arbitrary code execution as the compiling process;
   180	- spawn descendants, threads, and detached processes from that position;
   181	- attempt any system call the host permits;
   182	- write, rename, unlink, and link filesystem objects the host permits;
   183	- attempt to reach the network on any address family, including loopback and
   184	  unix-domain endpoints;
   185	- attempt to execute any file it can reach, including a file it just wrote;
   186	- exhaust CPU, memory, descriptors, processes, disk, and diagnostic output; and
   187	- attempt to outlive the operation.
   188	
   189	### 3.2 What the profile promises
   190	
   191	Under a qualified platform, an adversary in that position MUST NOT be able to:
   192	
   193	1. reach any network endpoint (section 5.1);
   194	2. modify the frozen source snapshot or the Go toolchain (section 5.2);
   195	3. write anywhere outside the operation-private build root (section 5.3);
   196	4. exceed the aggregate process, memory, disk, time, and output bounds, or
   197	   survive the operation (section 5.4);
   198	5. execute any program outside the exact fingerprinted allowlist (section 5.5);
   199	   or
   200	6. cause the operation to proceed to a compiler when any of 1 through 5 could
   201	   not be established (section 5.6).
   202	
   203	### 3.3 What the profile does not promise
   204	
   205	- It does not make adversarial Go source safe, and it does not claim the Go
   206	  toolchain is free of defects. The compile-only rules of `protocol/core.md`
   207	  sections 4.2 and 4.2.2 and of [`SECURITY.md`](../SECURITY.md) remain in force
   208	  and are not relaxed by containment.
   209	- It does not authenticate the compiled artifact. The artifact remains untrusted
   210	  package code, executed later under the consuming environment's own
   211	  authorization policy. `protocol/core.md` section 9.3 continues to govern.
   212	- It does not defend against an adversary who already runs as the manager's
   213	  operating-system principal, as an administrator or root, in the kernel, or
   214	  below the operating system. Such an adversary can replace the supervisor, the
   215	  worker, the markers, and the receipts.
   216	- It does not defend against a compromised or dishonest enforcement backend. A
   217	  backend is trusted computing base, which is why section 2.3 requires it to be
   218	  named.
   219	- It does not promise timing, cache, power, or other side-channel isolation
   220	  between the build domain and the host.
   221	- It does not promise that a qualified platform stays qualified: a host that
   222	  loses a capability between operations rejects the next operation rather than
   223	  degrading it.
   224	
   225	### 3.4 Trusted computing base
   226	
   227	The following are trusted, and a defect in any of them defeats the profile:
   228	
   229	- the installed manager parent, hardened supervisor, and domain-root worker
   230	  bytes, and the code that verifies their identities;
   231	- the domain session framing, authentication, nonce handling, and state machine;
   232	- the capability probes and the enforcement-backend adapters;
   233	- the operating-system or hypervisor primitives those adapters use;
   234	- the fingerprinted `go` launcher and the fingerprinted `GOROOT` tool
   235	  executables;
   236	- source and build-root canonicalization, fingerprinting, and input validation;
   237	- the operation-private roots, the artifact verifier, and the publication path;
   238	  and
   239	- the policy, cache, receipt, marker, and claim canonicalization code.
   240	
   241	## 4. Process graph and the build domain
   242	
   243	### 4.1 Fixed graph
   244	
   245	```text
   246	manager parent                        (trusted, outside the build domain)
   247	  -> identity-verified hardened supervisor
   248	                                      (trusted, outside the build domain)
   249	       -> domain-root worker          (first process INSIDE the build domain)
   250	            -> fingerprinted <GOROOT>/bin/go
   251	                 -> fingerprinted regular executables below
   252	                    <GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/
   253	```
   254	
   255	The graph has exactly five node kinds and no others. The supervisor exists
   256	because creating a containment object, applying its controls, and verifying them
   257	require operations that the contained domain MUST NOT be able to perform; a
   258	process cannot be both the creator of its own confinement and confined by it
   259	from its first instruction.
   260	
   261	The parent and the supervisor MAY be the same executable in two different fixed
   262	hidden modes, provided each mode's identity is verified independently. The
   263	supervisor and the worker MUST NOT be a shell, an interpreter invocation, a
   264	package file, a `PATH` lookup, a manifest value, a descriptor value, an
   265	environment value, or a user option. `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1's
   266	statement that no package input selects a program applies here unchanged and is
   267	extended by section 9.
   268	
   269	### 4.2 Domain membership
   270	
   271	Every process created by any process in the build domain MUST itself be in the
   272	build domain. Membership MUST NOT be renounceable from inside: a contained
   273	process MUST NOT be able to detach, re-parent out, daemonize out, join another
   274	domain, or create a process that outlives the domain.
   275	
   276	### 4.3 Session
   277	
   278	One hardened operation runs exactly one domain session, and the session performs
   279	exactly one `go list` and exactly one `go build`, using exactly the two argument
   280	vectors fixed by `protocol/core.md` section 4.2. In order, the session:
   281	
   282	1. proves the worker's identity to the parent and acknowledges a fresh session
   283	   nonce;
   284	2. performs the one `go list`;
   285	3. returns bounded output and waits;
   286	4. accepts exactly one authenticated build permit issued by the parent after the
   287	   parent has applied every dependency, containment, directive, and native-input
   288	   rejection of `protocol/core.md` section 4.2 and `profiles/manager.md` section
   289	   2.3 to the complete stream; and
   290	5. performs the one `go build`.
   291	
   292	Any other message, any repetition, any reordering, and any additional executable,
   293	shell, VCS, module download, generator, test, run, or tool request tears the
   294	session down without starting a compiler and reports
   295	`hardened_domain_protocol_invalid`.
   296	
   297	### 4.4 The session channel
   298	
   299	The domain session channel MUST be established before domain entry, MUST consist
   300	only of descriptors or handles the supervisor pre-opened and explicitly passed,
   301	and MUST NOT be a network endpoint, a filesystem path reachable from inside the
   302	domain, or any object the domain can re-open, re-bind, duplicate to another
   303	peer, or use to write outside the private build root. Establishing the channel
   304	MUST NOT weaken section 5.1.
   305	
   306	## 5. The six guarantees
   307	
   308	Each guarantee below is a kernel- or hypervisor-enforced property of the build
   309	domain. For each, the "not sufficient" list names mechanisms that a conforming
   310	implementation MUST NOT present as establishing the guarantee, because the
   311	contained adversary can defeat or change them.
   312	
   313	### 5.1 `total-network-denial`
   314	
   315	Every process in the build domain is denied every network operation: socket
   316	creation, bind, connect, listen, accept, sendto, sendmsg, and any equivalent, on
   317	every address family, including loopback, IPv4, IPv6, unix-domain endpoints that
   318	lead outside the domain, and any other transport the host provides. No inherited,
   319	pre-opened, or pre-connected endpoint survives domain entry.
   320	
   321	Not sufficient: `GOPROXY=off`, `GOVCS=*:off`, `GOFLAGS`, vendor-only module
   322	mode, an empty `PATH`, an unset proxy environment, a DNS blackhole, a userspace
   323	resolver stub, a firewall rule the domain can change, or a post-hoc check that
   324	no connection was observed.
   325	
   326	### 5.2 `read-only-source-and-toolchain`
   327	
   328	The frozen source snapshot and the fingerprinted `GOROOT` are presented to every
   329	process in the build domain through a view the kernel refuses to mutate. Write,
   330	create, rename, unlink, truncate, permission change, ownership change, extended
   331	attribute change, and hard-link creation against those views are denied
   332	regardless of the contained process's own credentials.
   333	
   334	Not sufficient: filesystem permissions or an access-control list the domain can
   335	change; a copy the manager promises not to write to; re-verifying the snapshot
   336	digest after the fact.
   337	
   338	Post-operation identity re-verification of the snapshot, the toolchain, the
   339	supervisor, and the worker remains REQUIRED by section 7, but it is a detection
   340	mechanism layered on top of this guarantee, never a substitute for it.
   341	
   342	### 5.3 `private-build-root-only-writes`
   343	
   344	Every mutating filesystem operation by every process in the build domain
   345	succeeds only below the operation-private build root, and is denied everywhere
   346	else. This covers writes, creates, renames, unlinks, links, permission and
   347	ownership changes, extended attributes, device-node creation, and filesystem
   348	IPC objects. Paths outside the declared views MUST NOT be reachable from inside
   349	the domain at all.
   350	
   351	The operation-private build root, the private user, configuration, cache,
   352	temporary, staging, and output roots, and every view boundary are resolved
   353	independently of package data.
   354	
   355	Not sufficient: a private temporary directory the domain can escape by absolute
   356	path; a `TMPDIR` value; a manager promise to write only to private roots; a
   357	post-hoc scan for unexpected files.
   358	
   359	### 5.4 `hard-aggregate-descendant-resource-bounds`
   360	
   361	Every bound below is over the whole build domain in aggregate, not per process,
   362	and covers every descendant:
   363	
   364	| Quantity | Bound |
   365	|---|---|
   366	| wall-clock time | aggregate deadline over the domain |
   367	| CPU time | aggregate limit over the domain |
   368	| resident and committed memory | aggregate limit over the domain |
   369	| live processes and threads | aggregate limit over the domain |
   370	| open descriptors or handles | aggregate limit over the domain |
   371	| bytes written below the private build root | aggregate limit over the domain |
   372	| combined standard output and standard error | aggregate limit over the domain |
   373	
   374	For each quantity, either the host accounts for and enforces the bound itself,
   375	or the supervisor accounts for it from outside the domain and enforces it by
   376	destroying the domain. Both forms are admissible, and exactly one property makes
   377	them equivalent: **no process in the domain can evade the accounting, prevent
   378	the enforcement, or survive it.** That property is supplied by
   379	`domain-membership-enforcement` and `domain-atomic-termination`, which is why
   380	section 6.2 requires both for this guarantee.
   381	
   382	Exceeding any bound MUST destroy the entire domain as a unit, not the offending
   383	process alone, and the operation MUST NOT return, publish, or report success
   384	while any domain member is alive.
   385	
   386	Not sufficient: a per-process `RLIMIT`; a parent-side deadline that kills only
   387	the direct child, or that a descendant can outlive by detaching; a periodic
   388	sweep for stray processes; an accounting-only control with no enforcement; a
   389	bound over a process group or session that a contained process can leave.
   390	
   391	This is the exact point where the portable profile stops. Its parent-enforced
   392	deadline and output bounds are supervisor-side accounting without unescapable
   393	membership or atomic termination, so a detached descendant escapes them.
   394	
   395	### 5.5 `exact-executable-allowlisting`
   396	
   397	The host denies execution of every program except the exact fingerprinted paths
   398	the manager selected: `<GOROOT>/bin/go` and the regular executables below
   399	`<GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/`. The allowlist is by exact path and
   400	verified identity. A file written inside the private build root MUST NOT be
   401	executable from inside the domain, and neither a shell, an interpreter, a
   402	dynamic loader invoked as a program, a VCS client, an external compiler,
   403	assembler or linker, nor any other host program may start.
   404	
   405	Not sufficient: an empty `PATH`; a manager promise to start nothing else; a
