     6	
     7	## Context
     8	
     9	Decision 0006 made the portable `manager-worker-v1` execution policy the single
    10	execution contract of protocol 1.0 and was explicit about what it does not
    11	provide. Six guarantees — `total-network-denial`,
    12	`read-only-source-and-toolchain`, `private-build-root-only-writes`,
    13	`hard-aggregate-descendant-resource-bounds`, `exact-executable-allowlisting`,
    14	and `fail-closed-capability-preflight` — were named, attributed to
    15	`STORY-260728-327soo`, forbidden to portable builds, and reserved together with
    16	an execution-policy identity, `hardened-worker-v1`, and a reserved cache key in
    17	`conformance/v1/vectors/go-host-execution-policy.json`.
    18	
    19	That reservation left three questions open. What exactly does each guarantee
    20	require of a host, in terms a reviewer can check and an adversarial test can
    21	falsify? What must be true before a compiler runs, and what happens when it is
    22	not? And how does hardened output stay distinguishable from portable output in
    23	every reusable artifact: cache entry, receipt, install marker, and conformance
    24	claim?
    25	
    26	Answering those questions before any native work starts is the point of this
    27	record. The alternative — writing a Linux sandbox first and deriving the
    28	contract from what that sandbox happened to achieve — is how "best-effort
    29	controls" become "guarantees" by accident, which is precisely the failure
    30	decision 0006 spent its length avoiding.
    31	
    32	The candidate `1.0.0-rc.5` suite is accepted and pinned: `conformance/v1` is
    33	hashed into `release/1.0.0-rc.5.json` and that digest is consumed downstream.
    34	Any hardened material inside that suite root would change the pin.
    35	
    36	## Decision
    37	
    38	The hardened profile is specified as an **additive, separately versioned**
    39	candidate, `hardened-1.0.0-rc.1`, with its own suite root, its own schemas, and
    40	its own release metadata:
    41	
    42	- `protocol/hardened-execution.md` and `profiles/manager-hardened.md` are the
    43	  normative documents;
    44	- `schemas/hardened/v1/` holds its schemas;
    45	- `conformance/hardened/v1/` is its suite root; and
    46	- `release/hardened-1.0.0-rc.1.json` pins that suite and records, read-only,
    47	  the rc.5 portable manifest digest it builds on.
    48	
    49	Nothing under `conformance/v1`, `schemas/v1`, or `release/1.0.0-rc.5.json`
    50	changes. The portable profile stays a complete, self-contained, weaker contract,
    51	and a manager may implement it and never implement this one.
    52	
    53	### The contract is all-or-nothing
    54	
    55	All six guarantees hold, or the operation rejects. There is no partial hardened
    56	mode, no best-effort guarantee, no warning-level degradation, and no silent
    57	fallback from hardened to portable. An operator who wants the portable contract
    58	selects the portable profile explicitly, and that operation is portable in every
    59	respect including its cache key.
    60	
    61	### Guarantees are stated as kernel refusals
    62	
    63	Each guarantee is defined as something the kernel or hypervisor refuses to do
    64	for every process in the build domain, and each definition carries an explicit
    65	"not sufficient" list naming the manager-side mechanisms that must not be
    66	presented as establishing it: configuration flags, filesystem permissions the
    67	domain can change, per-process resource limits, empty `PATH` values, post-hoc
    68	scans, and promises. That structure is what keeps the six names from drifting
    69	back into the portable meanings decision 0006 separated them from.
    70	
    71	### The graph gains one uncontained node
    72	
    73	```text
    74	manager parent                        (trusted, outside the build domain)
    75	  -> identity-verified hardened supervisor
    76	                                      (trusted, outside the build domain)
    77	       -> domain-root worker          (first process INSIDE the build domain)
    78	            -> fingerprinted <GOROOT>/bin/go
    79	                 -> fingerprinted regular executables below
    80	                    <GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/
    81	```
    82	
    83	Creating a containment object, applying its controls, and verifying them require
    84	operations the contained domain must not be able to perform. A process cannot be
    85	both the creator of its own confinement and confined by it from its first
    86	instruction, so the supervisor is uncontained and the worker is the first
    87	process inside the domain.
    88	
    89	### The failure boundary is before domain entry
    90	
    91	Fifteen ordered phases are normative. Profile selection, platform qualification,
    92	capability probing, trusted-computing-base verification, domain establishment,
    93	and an in-domain guarantee self-test all complete before domain entry, and
    94	domain entry precedes `go list`. So every capability rejection happens before
    95	any package byte is read by any Go process — strictly earlier than the portable
    96	boundary, which rejects before the worker but after the portable graph has been
    97	selected.
