   489	Candidate primitive bindings, and the classes that currently block
   490	qualification, are:
   491	
   492	- **`linux-namespace-seccomp-v1`** — user, mount, PID, network, IPC, UTS, and
   493	  cgroup namespaces for domain membership; read-only bind mounts for the source
   494	  and toolchain views; an empty network namespace; `seccomp-BPF` with
   495	  `no_new_privs` for the execution allowlist and residual syscall denial; a
   496	  size-bounded private filesystem, such as a size-limited `tmpfs` or a
   497	  quota-backed subvolume, for the aggregate write-byte bound; and cgroup v2
   498	  `pids.max`, `memory.max`, and CPU limits with `cgroup.kill` for the remaining
   499	  aggregate bounds and atomic termination. No class is currently identified as
   500	  unreachable on this platform; qualification nonetheless requires native
   501	  adversarial evidence and a declared minimum kernel version and required
   502	  configuration, and is owned by `TASK-260728-3ihgfq` for Curator and
   503	  `TASK-260728-ns5yk7` for csk.
   504	- **`macos-sandbox-v1`** — a `(deny default)` sandbox profile with explicit
   505	  `file-read*` allowances for the snapshot and `GOROOT`, `file-write*` allowance
   506	  restricted to the private build root, `(deny network*)`, and
   507	  `(deny process-exec*)` with exact-path allowances; process-group and session
   508	  teardown; and `RLIMIT_*` for per-process bounds. Blocking classes today:
   509	  `domain-membership-enforcement`, `domain-atomic-termination`, and
   510	  `aggregate-resource-bounds`, because the platform exposes no unescapable
   511	  per-operation process domain — a contained process can leave the process group
   512	  or session — and no aggregate private storage, memory, or process-count
   513	  accounting. That is the same `no-private-aggregate-domain` finding the portable
