   688	`execution_policy: "hardened-worker-v1"`.
   689	
   690	Consequently, for one identical source, command, target, and toolchain, three
   691	inputs produce three different keys and none of them aliases another:
   692	
   693	| Input | Execution policy | Key |
   694	|---|---|---|
   695	| pre-revision rc.4 candidate | absent | `sha256:3fcd714a40e8918eb67dbd35d435875dcce6c9047da811a1fa26626e5e57be48` |
   696	| portable | `manager-worker-v1` | `sha256:529370122ae11e2e961d5265b1a020e046bcd43165b2eb96b05e73a51187ac9b` |
   697	| hardened | `hardened-worker-v1` | `sha256:13736230d33ce59de7f7323dcd4cffd510655ad8dabd5ee9e8b6cb182ec70037` |
   698	
   699	A reader MUST treat an entry whose execution policy is not the policy it
   700	implements for this operation as a miss. A hardened operation MUST NOT accept a
   701	portable entry, a portable operation MUST NOT accept a hardened entry, and
   702	neither MUST upgrade, downgrade, re-label, or re-sign an existing entry in
   703	place. A manager that implements both profiles MUST keep their entries logically
   704	disjoint and MUST revalidate the protected-cache boundary of `protocol/core.md`
   705	section 9.3 for each profile independently.
   706	
   707	Neither the hardened capability-evidence record, nor the TCB record, nor the
   708	enforcement backend, nor the platform participates in the cache key or in the
   709	hit comparison.
   710	
   711	### 8.2 Receipts
   712	
   713	A hardened local `go-v1` build writes **build receipt schema 3**. A hardened
   714	external `go-repository-v1` build writes **build receipt schema 4**. Both keep
   715	the exact receipt structure of `protocol/core.md` sections 9.1 and 9.2 — schema
   716	version, logical cache key, complete build input, and artifact path, SHA-256, and
   717	byte length — and both carry the hardened execution-policy identity inside the
   718	input.
   719	
   720	Receipt schemas 1 and 2 remain exactly as candidate `1.0.0-rc.5` defines them
   721	and MUST NOT be widened. A reader that does not implement the hardened profile
   722	MUST reject receipt schema 3 and 4 as unsupported receipt identities under
   723	`protocol/core.md` section 9.3; it MUST NOT parse them leniently, convert them,
   724	or treat them as schema 1 or 2.
   725	
   726	`receipt_sha256` is computed exactly as in `protocol/core.md` section 9.1 over
   727	the exact stored canonical bytes.
   728	
   729	The TCB record, the enforcement backend, the platform, and the capability
   730	evidence MUST NOT appear in a receipt. A receipt is shared, reusable, host-
   731	independent state; host mechanism is not.
   732	
   733	### 8.3 Why host mechanism is not hashed
   734	
   735	Putting the enforcement backend, the platform, or the TCB digest into the build
   736	input would make one source, toolchain, and contract produce a different key on
   737	every host and after every manager update, without adding a guarantee a reader
   738	may rely on, and it would invite a reader to treat a cache key as capability
   739	proof. `decisions/0006` rejected exactly that for portable capability evidence,
   740	and the same reasoning applies here. The contract is all-or-nothing: under a
   741	qualified platform every guarantee holds, so the artifact contract is identical
   742	regardless of which qualified backend produced it.
   743	
   744	The corresponding risk — a host silently losing a capability — is answered by
   745	section 7, which re-probes and re-establishes every guarantee on every
   746	operation, not by cache-key fragmentation.
   747	
   748	### 8.4 Install markers
   749	
   750	A hardened installation writes **install marker schema 4**. Its build records
   751	carry `execution_policy: "hardened-worker-v1"`, the profile identity, and the
   752	TCB record, in addition to the receipt schema version, cache key,
   753	`receipt_sha256`, artifact digest, and artifact path.
   754	
   755	Marker schema 4 is written for a hardened installation of a manifest schema-6
   756	or schema-7 package alike, so `skill_schema_version` is `6` or `7`. That
   757	replaces the portable rule of `profiles/manager.md` section 2.2 — marker 2 for
   758	schema 1 through 6, marker 3 for schema 7 — for hardened operations only,
   759	because a marker 2 or 3 build record cannot express the hardened identities.
   760	Manifest schemas 1 through 5 have no build commands, so they never produce a
   761	hardened marker.
   762	
   763	Marker schemas 1, 2, and 3 keep their exact candidate `1.0.0-rc.5` shapes and
   764	MUST NOT be widened. A marker-v4 build record MUST NOT appear in a marker of
   765	schema 1, 2, or 3, and a portable build record MUST NOT carry a hardened
   766	profile or TCB identity.
   767	
   768	A marker MUST NOT record a hardened build record for an operation that did not
   769	establish the hardened domain. Doing so is `hardened_profile_claim_forbidden`.
   770	
   771	### 8.5 Conformance claims
   772	
   773	A hardened claim uses **conformance claim schema 4**. It carries the hardened
   774	profile identity, the execution-policy identity `hardened-worker-v1`, the
   775	enforcement backend per operating system, the TCB record, the capability
   776	inventory version, and the hardened suite digest.
   777	
   778	Claim schema 3 admits only `manager-worker-v1` and therefore cannot express a
   779	hardened claim; it MUST NOT be widened. Claim schema 4 admits only
   780	`hardened-worker-v1` and therefore cannot express a portable claim. The two are
   781	structurally disjoint in both directions.
   782	
   783	A claim-4 operating system MUST be a platform whose declaration in section 6.3
   784	is `qualified` in the revision the claim names. Because every declaration in
   785	`hardened-profile-v1` is `unqualified`, **no conforming claim-4 document can be
   786	emitted against this revision**, and the candidate metadata records that as
   787	`claims_emitted: []`.
   788	
   789	## 9. Package-influence exclusions
   790	

codex
Two review-critical contradictions have surfaced in the normative text, and I’m now checking whether the executable vectors/schemas reinforce or repair them:

- the TCB is explicitly excluded from cache-hit and receipt identity, despite the task DoD requiring hardened TCB/profile identity binding across cache, receipt, marker, and claim state;
- the “self-test inside the build domain” is ordered before “domain entry,” even though the domain-root worker is defined as the first process inside that domain.

I’m validating these against the generated conformance authority before deciding the verdict.
